h1. Event Pump - Multi-Tenant Overview (SPEC v1.2)

{{>toc}}

_For internal use - platform team. One process, one Postgres, N tenants. Every tenant carries its own tokens, tracking plan, routing map, per-destination rename map, and destination credentials. Nothing is shared at the plan level; the tenant id is stamped on every domain row so isolation is enforced by the schema and the type system - not by convention. Examples below use *app A* and *app B* as the two tenants._

----

h2. Key Terms

|_.Term|_.What it means|
|*Tenant*|A first-party app served by the same Event Pump process. Identified by @app_id@ (values in the smoke: any two strings, e.g. app A, app B).|
|*app_id*|Primary tenant identifier. Resolved from the bearer token (HTTP) or supplied as the first argument to @emit_event@ (SQL). Never producer-supplied over the wire on the HTTP path.|
|*Client token*|Bearer sent by client SDKs on @POST /v1/*@. One tenant may have several (for token rotation).|
|*Internal token*|Bearer sent by server producers on @POST /internal/v1/*@ and DSR endpoints. One per tenant.|
|*Tracking plan*|Per-tenant schema: which events exist, their origin (@client@/@server@), which destinations they route to, and per-destination rename maps. Lives in the tenant's JSONC file, parsed into @TrackingPlan@.|
|*Routing*|The destinations list on each event - controls the fan-out into @events_delivery@.|
|*Mapping*|Per-destination event-name and property-key rewrites - controls what the payload looks like on the wire. SPEC §6.2 R1-R7.|
|*Pipeline*|One @(app_id, destination)@ pair inside the worker: own claim loop, own HTTP client, own circuit breaker, own metrics labels.|

----

h2. 1. Tenant Configuration

One JSONC file per tenant in the directory pointed to by @EP_TENANTS_DIR@. Reference file in the repo: @deploy/tenants/zainmart.example.jsonc@.

*Where in the code:*

* @server/src/EventPump/Config/TenantConfig.cs@ - the record: @AppId@, @ClientTokens@, @InternalToken@, @Plan@, per-destination @Enabled@ / @Endpoint@ / credentials.
* @server/src/EventPump/Config/TenantRegistry.cs@ - the loader: @TenantRegistry.Load()@ scans @EP_TENANTS_DIR@ and builds three dictionaries: @_byToken@, @_byInternalToken@, @_byAppId@. There is *no global tracking plan* - each @TenantConfig@ holds its own @Plan@.

Skeleton (only the notable fields shown):

<pre>
{
  "app_id": "app_a",
  "client_tokens":  ["app_a_web_tok", "app_a_mobile_tok"],
  "internal_token": "app_a_internal_secret",
  "cookie_domain":  ".app-a.example",
  "cors_origins":   ["https://www.app-a.example"],
  "rate_limit":     { "permits": 600, "window_seconds": 60 },

  "attributes": { /* SPEC 6.1 allowlist */ },
  "events":     { /* SPEC 8 routing map */ },
  "destinations": {
    "ga4": { "events": { "product_viewed": { "name": "view_item",
                                             "properties": { "product_id": "item_id" } } } }
  },
  "destination_config": {
    "ga4":       { "enabled": true,  "endpoint": "...", "api_secret": "...", ... },
    "amplitude": { "enabled": true,  "endpoint": "...", "api_key": "...", ... },
    "moengage":  { "enabled": true,  "endpoint": "...", "api_key": "...", ... },
    "adjust":    { "enabled": true,  "endpoint": "...", "app_token": "...", ... },
    "meta":      { "enabled": false, ... }
  }
}
</pre>

*Global env* (shared across tenants): @EP_DB_CONNSTRING@, @EP_LISTEN@, @EP_INTERNAL_LISTEN@, @EP_METRICS_LISTEN@, @EP_TENANTS_DIR@, @EP_RETENTION_DAYS@, @EP_WORKER_*@, @EP_IP_MODE@, @EP_DOCS@.

*Per tenant* (all other knobs): tokens, cookie domain, CORS, rate limits, plan, destination endpoints, credentials, attribute gates.

----

h2. 2. Upstream Flow - Producer to Storage

Three producer paths. Each identifies the tenant differently, but the outcome is the same: the tenant id is resolved before validation runs, and validation uses only *that tenant's* plan.

{{plantuml(svg)
@startuml
skinparam BackgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam RectangleBorderColor #534AB7
skinparam RectangleBackgroundColor #EEEDFE
skinparam ArrowColor #534AB7
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #534AB7
skinparam DatabaseBackgroundColor #EEEDFE

rectangle "Client SDK app A" as CA
rectangle "Client SDK app B" as CB
rectangle "Server producer HTTP" as SRV
rectangle "Server producer SQL" as SQL

rectangle "POST /v1/*" as API
rectangle "POST /internal/v1/*" as INT
rectangle "emit_event p_app_id" as FN

rectangle "TenantRegistry lookup" as REG
rectangle "EventValidation.ValidateBatch tenant.Plan" as VAL
rectangle "EventStore.InsertBatchAsync app_id" as ST

database "Postgres events_outbox and events_delivery" as DB

CA --> API : Bearer app_a tok
CB --> API : Bearer app_b tok
SRV --> INT : Bearer app_x internal
SQL --> FN  : 'app_a' or 'app_b'

API --> REG
INT --> REG
FN  --> REG
REG --> VAL
VAL --> ST
ST  --> DB
@enduml
}}

*Producer paths (as code):*

|_.Path|_.Tenant resolver|_.Failure mode|
| @POST /v1/*@ (client SDK) | @ApiApp.ResolveClientTenant@ -> @TenantRegistry.ByClientToken(token)@ | Unknown token = 401 Unauthorized |
| @POST /internal/v1/*@ (server HTTP) | @ApiApp.ResolveInternalTenant@ -> @TenantRegistry.ByInternalToken(token)@ | Unknown token = 401 Unauthorized |
| @emit_event(p_app_id, ...)@ (SQL) | Function's first argument, looked up in @event_registry (app_id, event_name)@ | Unknown pair = @RAISE EXCEPTION 'unknown server event_name for app_id'@ inside the producer's own transaction |

Two client SDKs (app A and app B) reach the same @/v1/*@ endpoint. Their tokens are different, so @TenantRegistry.ByClientToken@ maps each to its own @TenantConfig@. The API handler passes @tenant.Plan@ to @EventValidation@ - so the same handler validates app A's batch against app A's plan and app B's batch against app B's plan.

----

h2. 3. Inside the Pipeline - How A and B Stay Separated

Once the tenant is resolved, @app_id@ becomes a mandatory column on every row written for that request. Composite PKs on the tables whose identifier space is per-tenant.

{{plantuml(svg)
@startuml
skinparam BackgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #EEEDFE
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #534AB7
skinparam ArrowColor #534AB7
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #CECBF6

start
:Bearer token in;
if (Token known?) then (yes)
  :Resolve tenant A or B via TenantRegistry;
  :Validate batch against tenant.Plan;
  if (Event name in tenant.Plan?) then (yes)
    :InsertBatchAsync stamps app_id on every row;
    :JOIN event_registry on app_id and event_name;
    :Fan out one events_delivery row per destination;
    stop
  else (no)
    :Reject with unknown_event_name;
    stop
  endif
else (no)
  :Return 401 unauthorized;
  stop
endif
@enduml
}}

*Schema-level isolation seams (migration 0009):*

|_.Table|_.Primary key|_.Why|
| @events_outbox@ | @(received_at, id)@ + @app_id@ column | Tenant stamped on every event; JOINs narrow by @app_id@ |
| @events_delivery@ | @(received_at, event_ref, destination)@ + @app_id@ column | Worker claim filters @WHERE destination = $1 AND app_id = $4@ |
| @events_dedupe@ | @event_id@ + @app_id@ column | UUIDs are globally unique; @app_id@ retained for observability |
| @identity_registry@ | @session_key@ + @app_id@ column | UUIDs; queries narrow by @app_id@ for defense in depth |
| @first_seen@ | *composite* @(app_id, anonymous_id)@ | Each tenant gets its own once-ever @first_visit@ |
| @event_registry@ | *composite* @(app_id, event_name)@ | Same event name, different routing per tenant |
| @user_attributes@ | *composite* @(app_id, user_id)@ | User id spaces don't collide across tenants |

*Consequences at the source level:*

* App A can declare event @order_placed@ and app B can declare event @ride_requested@. They never coexist in a shared registry because @event_registry.PRIMARY KEY = (app_id, event_name)@.
* If A's plan has @order_placed@ and B's plan does not, a request from B's SDK with @event_name: "order_placed"@ is rejected with @unknown_event_name@ - the validator sees only B's plan.
* Same @user_id "42"@ exists in both A and B - two separate rows in @user_attributes@ because the PK is @(app_id, user_id)@.

----

h2. 4. Downstream Flow - Worker to Destinations

The worker constructs one *independent pipeline per @(app_id, destination)@ pair* at boot in @SenderFactory.Create@. Own HTTP client, own claim loop, own circuit breaker, own metrics labels. One tenant's outage cannot pause another.

{{plantuml(svg)
@startuml
skinparam BackgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam RectangleBorderColor #0F6E56
skinparam RectangleBackgroundColor #E1F5EE
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #534AB7
skinparam DatabaseBackgroundColor #EEEDFE
skinparam CloudBackgroundColor #FFF9E8
skinparam CloudBorderColor #D69D00
skinparam ArrowColor #0F6E56

database "events_delivery keyed by app_id and destination" as DB

package "App A pipelines" {
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = A AND destination = ga4" as A1
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = A AND destination = amplitude" as A2
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = A AND destination = moengage" as A3
}

package "App B pipelines" {
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = B AND destination = ga4" as B1
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = B AND destination = amplitude" as B2
  rectangle "claim WHERE app_id = B AND destination = moengage" as B3
}

rectangle "Ga4Sender for A applies A rename map" as SAG
rectangle "AmplitudeSender for A" as SAA
rectangle "MoEngageSender for A" as SAM

rectangle "Ga4Sender for B applies B rename map" as SBG
rectangle "AmplitudeSender for B" as SBA
rectangle "MoEngageSender for B" as SBM

cloud "GA4 endpoint A" as EAG
cloud "Amplitude A" as EAA
cloud "MoEngage A" as EAM

cloud "GA4 endpoint B" as EBG
cloud "Amplitude B" as EBA
cloud "MoEngage B" as EBM

DB --> A1
DB --> A2
DB --> A3
A1 --> SAG
A2 --> SAA
A3 --> SAM
SAG --> EAG
SAA --> EAA
SAM --> EAM

DB --> B1
DB --> B2
DB --> B3
B1 --> SBG
B2 --> SBA
B3 --> SBM
SBG --> EBG
SBA --> EBA
SBM --> EBM
@enduml
}}

*Where in the code:*

* @server/src/EventPump/Senders/SenderFactory.cs@ - iterates @tenants.All@ and adds one sender per enabled @(tenant × destination)@ pair. Filter is @if (tenant.Ga4Enabled) senders.Add(new Ga4Sender(tenant, ...))@.
* @server/src/EventPump/Worker/DeliveryWorker.cs@ - one @RunPipelineAsync(sender, stopToken)@ task per sender. Claim SQL: @WHERE destination = $1 AND app_id = $4@. Each pipeline has its own @Breaker@ instance keyed on @(appId, destination)@.
* Each @Ga4Sender@ / @AmplitudeSender@ / etc. holds a @_tenant@ field of type @TenantConfig@ - never a shared config.

*Observability (Prometheus labels):*

* @deliveries_total{app_id, destination, status}@
* @outbox_pending{app_id, destination}@
* @circuit_state{app_id, destination}@
* @delivery_latency_seconds{app_id, destination}@

*Behavioural consequences:*

* App A's GA4 breaker opens only its @{app_id=A, destination=ga4}@ pipeline. App B's GA4 keeps flowing.
* Setting @destination_config.moengage.enabled = false@ in A's file removes only A's MoEngage sender at boot. B's MoEngage continues.
* Adding a tenant is: drop a JSONC file, restart. New pipelines light up on boot.

----

h2. 5. Routing and Mapping - Same Event, Different Wire

The tracking plan carries both:

* *Routing* - the @destinations@ list on each event. Drives fan-out into @events_delivery@.
* *Mapping* - the @destinations.<dest>.events.<x>@ block. Rewrites event name and property keys on the wire.

Both are per tenant. Applied inside the sender at delivery time via @plan.ResolveEventName()@ and @plan.ResolvePropertiesJson()@ - where @plan@ is the sender's *bound* tenant plan, injected in the ctor.

h3. 5.1 Routing (per tenant)

<pre>
// A's plan: product_viewed goes to three destinations
"product_viewed": { "destinations": ["ga4", "amplitude", "moengage"], ... }

// B's plan: same event, routed to only one
"product_viewed": { "destinations": ["amplitude"], ... }
</pre>

Fan-out happens in @EventStore.InsertBatchSql@ (see @server/src/EventPump/Data/EventStore.cs@) via @JOIN event_registry r ON r.app_id = $10 AND r.event_name = o.event_name@ followed by @CROSS JOIN LATERAL unnest(r.destinations)@. One row per destination in the list.

h3. 5.2 Mapping (per tenant × destination)

Given the canonical event @product_viewed@ with properties @{ product_id: "sku-1", price: 9.99 }@:

|_.Tenant|_.GA4 wire|_.Amplitude wire|_.MoEngage wire|
| *app A* | name = @view_item@ / params.item_id = @sku-1@ / params.value = @9.99@ | event_type = @Product Viewed@ / event_properties.product_id = @sku-1@ | action = @Product Viewed@ / attributes.product_id = @sku-1@ |
| *app B* | name = @b_product_view@ / params.item_ref = @sku-1@ | event_type = @PRODUCT_VIEW_B@ / event_properties.sku = @sku-1@ | action = @B Product View@ / attributes.sku_ref = @sku-1@ |

h3. 5.3 Rename map rules (SPEC §6.2)

|_.Rule|_.Meaning|
| *R1 / R2* | Destination-specific @name:@ overrides the canonical event name, else the canonical name is used. |
| *R3* | Property key rename: @{canonical_key: destination_key}@. |
| *Allowlist* | When a rename map is declared for an event under a destination, only listed canonical keys are forwarded; *unlisted keys are dropped*. Each tenant curates exactly what leaves the perimeter. |
| *R6* | No @name:@ rename allowed under @adjust@ (Adjust identifies events by token, not name). Enforced at plan load - boot fails. |
| *R7* | Every event in a rename map must exist in the top-level @events@ block, and its destination must be listed in that event's @destinations@ array. Enforced at plan load. |

*Where in the code:*

* @server/src/EventPump/Config/TrackingPlan.cs@ - @ResolveEventName()@ and @ResolvePropertiesJson()@ do the rewrites. Plan-load validation for R6/R7 is in the static @Parse()@ method.
* Each sender (@Ga4Sender.cs@, @AmplitudeSender.cs@, @MoEngageSender.cs@, @AdjustSender.cs@, @MetaCapiSender.cs@) calls both methods with the incoming @DeliveryItem.EventName@ and its bound tenant's plan.

----

h2. 6. Ops Cheat Sheet

|_.I want to...|_.Change|
| Add a tenant | Drop a new @.jsonc@ in @EP_TENANTS_DIR@, restart api + worker |
| Rotate a client token | Add the new one to @client_tokens@, roll SDKs, remove the old |
| Turn off MoEngage for app A only | @destination_config.moengage.enabled: false@ in A's file |
| Stop routing @event_x@ to MoEngage for A | Remove @"moengage"@ from @events.event_x.destinations@ in A's file |
| Change GA4 event name for A only | @destinations.ga4.events.event_x.name: "..."@ in A's file |
| Different Adjust token per tenant, same event | @events.event_x.adjust_token@ per tenant |
| Per-tenant rate limit | @rate_limit: { permits, window_seconds }@ |
| DSR erase for app A | @DELETE /internal/v1/user_attributes/app_a/{user_id}@ with A's internal token |

Restart api + worker after any tenant-file change - the registry is built once at boot, not hot-reloaded.

*Note on turning off a destination for one tenant only:* setting @enabled: false@ removes the pipeline at boot, so any rows already queued in @events_delivery@ for that tenant × destination will sit in @pending@ forever. To do a clean shutdown, also remove that destination from every event's @destinations@ array so nothing new fans out to it.
